self._confirm_counter  # Read\nself._confirm_counter += 1      # Increment (not 
atomic!)\nself._confirm_futures[req_id] = future\n```\n\nTwo concurrent async 
tasks could:\n1. Both read the same counter value (e.g., 0)\n2. Both increment 
it (to 1, then 2)  \n3. Both use the same ID (0), causing one future to 
overwrite another\n\nThe fix uses Python's built-in `id()` function which 
returns a unique integer for each object's lifetime:\n```python\nreq_id = 
id(future)  # Always unique for this future\nself._confirm_futures[req_id] = 
future\n```\n\nThis eliminates the race condition entirely without needing locks
or atomic operations."})

